For the S-box functions, translate the C code EXACTLY - it's the Boyar-Peralta circuit with variables x0-x7, y1-y21, z0-z17, t0-t67, s0-s7. The 64-bit version uses u64, the 32-bit version uses u32. Both have identical logic.

For haraka512_perm mix states, the bit manipulation is:
for j in 0..8 {
    let tmp_q = q[j];
    q[j] = (tmp_q & 0x0001000100010001) << 5
         | (tmp_q & 0x0002000200020002) << 12
         | (tmp_q & 0x0004000400040004) >> 1
         | (tmp_q & 0x0008000800080008) << 6
         | (tmp_q & 0x0020002000200020) << 9
         | (tmp_q & 0x0040004000400040) >> 4
         | (tmp_q & 0x0080008000800080) << 3
         | (tmp_q & 0x2100210021002100) >> 5
         | (tmp_q & 0x0210021002100210) << 2
         | (tmp_q & 0x0800080008000800) << 4
         | (tmp_q & 0x1000100010001000) >> 12
         | (tmp_q & 0x4000400040004000) >> 10
         | (tmp_q & 0x8400840084008400) >> 3;
}

For haraka256 mix states:
for j in 0..8 {
    let tmp_q = q[j];
    q[j] = (tmp_q & 0x81818181)
         | (tmp_q & 0x02020202) << 1
         | (tmp_q & 0x04040404) << 2
         | (tmp_q & 0x08080808) << 3
         | (tmp_q & 0x10101010) >> 3
         | (tmp_q & 0x20202020) >> 2
         | (tmp_q & 0x40404040) >> 1;
}

For shift_rows (u64 version):
q[i] = (x & 0x000000000000FFFF)
     | ((x & 0x00000000FFF00000) >> 4) | ((x & 0x00000000000F0000) << 12)
     | ((x & 0x0000FF0000000000) >> 8) | ((x & 0x000000FF00000000) << 8)
     | ((x & 0xF000000000000000) >> 12) | ((x & 0x0FFF000000000000) << 4);

For shift_rows32 (u32 version):
q[i] = (x & 0x000000FF)
     | ((x & 0x0000FC00) >> 2) | ((x & 0x00000300) << 6)
     | ((x & 0x00F00000) >> 4) | ((x & 0x000F0000) << 4)
     | ((x & 0xC0000000) >> 6) | ((x & 0x3F000000) << 2);

For the sponge inc_absorb, the state s_inc is [u8; 65] where s_inc[64] tracks the position. The finalize pads with 0x1F and sets high bit of last rate byte.

Make sure all wrapping arithmetic uses .wrapping_shl, .wrapping_shr, .wrapping_add etc or just use Wrapping types. Actually since these are all bitwise ops (^, &, |, <<, >>) on unsigned types, Rust handles them fine. Just make sure shifts don't overflow - use explicit masks.

